3. _What explains any difference in profitability between the two customers? What are the limitations, if any, to the estimates of the profitability of the two customers?_


Although both customers have the same warehouse handling and storage (200 $times$ \$52) as well as interest rate (10% of average accounts receivable), they have significant differences. The difference in profitability between the two customors can be explained by:
- All 200 of A's cartons went by commercial freight; for B, 150 went by freight and 50 by desktop delivery, so B's freight is lower (150 $times$ \$6).
- All desktop deliveries (25 $times$ \$220) were for customer B.
- Customer A had a total of 6 manual orders at \$10 each, 60 lines at \$4 each as well as 6 EDI orders at \$5 each. For a total of \$330.
- Customer B, on the other hand, did not have EDI orders, with only 100 manual orders at \$10 each and 180 lines at \$4 each. For a total of \$1,720.
- Although they have the same interest rate (10%), the interest cost is very different. Slow payment (90+ days vs about 30) leaves \$30,000 of receivables outstanding vs \$9,000, costing \$3,000 vs \$900, for B and A, respectively.
- Desktop deliveries cost \$5,500 for B while customer A had 0.
- Customer B has 100 small manual orders for a cost of \$1,720 vs \$330 for customer A's 12 larger orders, half of them (6) placed by EDI.

The current method shows that customer B as the more profitable than customer B (\$6,250 vs \$5,250). Activity-based costing reverses this (\$5.170 vs -\$2.520).

*Limitations*

- Activity splits and time per task come from interviews and a few days of observation at one distribution center, so the estimates may be questionned.
- There is no clear way to allocate general and selling (G&S) costs between customers.
- The case treats every carton as costing the same to handle and ship, regardless of weight or distance.
- Desktop delivery is costed per delivery, not by distance or cartons delivered.

